//? Title: Fibonacci Coding: Exact Weights, Strict Frames and Actual Word Spans
//? Subtitle: Zeckendorf digits, full-range uint64 round trips and the limits of delimiter-based recovery
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Represent a sequence of positive integers without transmitting a separate
length for each value. A decoder must identify word boundaries and recover
every value exactly. Fibonacci coding uses nonconsecutive Fibonacci weights
and a final one-bit terminator. We start with the six-bit encoding of ten,
retain the original codewords, round trip and length comparison, then extend
the native codec to every positive uint64 value. Actual word spans and work
counts come from the encoder and strict whole-frame parser. Malformed tails,
width overflow, bit-flip experiments and explicit capture limits distinguish
self-delimiting framing from error detection, correction and recovery.
)

\toc

> Ten is eight plus two

Use coding weights W0=1,W1=2 and W(i+2)=W(i+1)+Wi. Ten is eight plus
two, so its ascending-weight payload is 01001. Append one terminating bit
to obtain 010011. Its final two ones form the delimiter: the first is the
highest payload digit, and the second is the appended terminator. This
overlap matters when labelling word spans and counting work.

The native trace below captures the actual output and its spans in the same
pass. The figure reads its bits directly and obtains the coding weights
from datamunge. The reusable bit-text renderer has the historical name
exp_golomb_bit_string but simply prints any vector<bool>; it performs no
coding or decoding.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fibonacci_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
