> Why greedy digits and the delimiter work

Choose the largest Wk≤n. Since n<W(k+1)=Wk+W(k−1), subtracting Wk
leaves less than W(k−1), so the next weight cannot be selected. Repeating
therefore gives nonconsecutive selected weights. Existence follows by
induction on the remaining positive integer. For uniqueness, the largest
sum using nonconsecutive weights below Wk is Wk−1; two distinct such
representations cannot disagree at their largest selected weight and still
have equal sums. This is the Zeckendorf representation for the weights1,2,... .

The last payload digit is one and its predecessor, if present, is zero.
There is no payload11; appending one introduces a first11 exactly at the
word end. From a known frame start, a decoder reads payload weights until
that pair, emits one value and resets its weight index and previous-bit
state. A longer word cannot begin with an entire shorter word, because
that would already contain the first terminating11. This establishes a
prefix code and unique parsing from the supplied start. It does not by
itself establish error correction or synchronization from an arbitrary bit.

If Wk≤n<W(k+1), payload length is k+1 and complete length k+2. Growth
Wk=Theta(phi^k) gives length log_phi(n)+O(1), about1.44042log2(n)+O(1).
The native length API uses exact integer weights, not rounded logarithms.

> Keep the original examples; compare domains explicitly

The original eight codewords and ten-value round trip are retained below.
The original length table compared positive Fibonacci input n with
nonnegative Exp-Golomb0 input n. Both calls are valid, but their integer
domains start differently. An additional fair-positive mapping compares
Fibonacci n with Exp-Golomb0(n−1). The latter uses one bit at n=1 while
Fibonacci uses two, so compactness is distribution- and mapping-dependent.
The plot uses log2(n) only as a display coordinate; every length is an
exact native count.

