> Full uint64 coverage and strict parsing

There are92 coding weights representable in uint64: W0=1 through
W91=12200160415121876738. The next recurrence value exceeds uint64.
The new core stores those exact weights once as an immutable fixed array;
it never forms the overflowing next weight. UINT64_MAX needs92 payload
digits and one terminator, for93 bits. The parser recognizes a terminating
one before requesting another weight, and checks value sums before addition.

The old encoder grew an unchecked Fibonacci vector until a weight exceeded
n, which cannot happen when n=UINT64_MAX after unsigned wrapping. The old
decoder used an even shorter table: the valid word with90 zero digits
followed by11 already indexes beyond its logical table. A hardened-library
probe confirms that violation; a longer malformed word triggers a heap
read overflow under ASAN. The replacement checks both the index and sum.

Whole-frame parsing now rejects unfinished tails, including a complete
word followed by a lone zero. Those bits are not implicit padding. Zero
is outside the positive code domain; it is rejected before append mutation.
The old zero encoder produced a lone1, which decoded to no value. The
following program shows valid maximum-width round trips and explicit errors.

