> Actual spans, work and owned history

The encoder and strict parser share their ordinary/work/trace kernels;
there is no explanatory codec replay. Work counts complete values, actual
payload bits and actual single terminating bits. bit_visits counts only
emitted or consumed positions; preflight, copying, allocation, table lookups
and greedy candidate checks are excluded. It is not a CPU-operation count
or timing measurement. Ordinary encoding uses O(N+B) word work and O(B)
output; parsing uses O(B) word work and O(N) output, with fixed scratch.

A trace owns B bit positions, N values and N whole span records, giving
logical capture C=B+2N. Scalar metadata, container capacity and headers,
allocators and runtime temporaries are excluded: this is not peak RAM.
Encoding checks complete C before copies/emission. Decoding admits B
before copying its input, then two entries before each completed value/span
append. Default quota is1000000; zero disables only that optional quota.
No partial trace is returned on any error. Ordinary/work APIs store no
optional history and have no history quota. The packed-bool container does
not specify byte serialization, endianness, padding or outer framing.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fibonacci_capture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
